<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ralph Abercromby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sir Ralph Abercromby</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Ralph Abercromby was a Scottish general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. In 1797 he led the British expedition that captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, beginning British rule there. He was mortally wounded in 1801 at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, where his army defeated the French forces left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ralph-abercromby.jpg" alt="Ralph Abercromby" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After John Hoppner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Ralph_Abercromby_by_John_Hoppner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ralph Abercromby was born on 7 October 1734 at Menstrie Castle, Clackmannanshire, the eldest surviving son of the lawyer George Abercromby, and spent much of his childhood at Tullibody House. He attended Rugby School, studied moral and natural philosophy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, and continued civil law at Leipzig University with a view to becoming an advocate. On his return he chose the army instead and became a cornet in the 3rd Dragoon Guards in March 1756.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, where he studied the methods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, and sat in Parliament for Clackmannanshire from 1774. He sympathised with the Patriot cause in the American War of Independence and served in Ireland rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war in 1793 he commanded a brigade under the Duke of York in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and was wounded at Fort St Andries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a> in 1794.</p><p>Appointed Commander-in-Chief of the Leeward and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> in 1795, he crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julien-fedon">Julien Fédon</a>'s rebellion on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> at the cost of 566 men and put down the rising on Saint Vincent in 1796. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> he secured Demerara and Essequibo, but an assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-puerto-rico">San Juan, Puerto Rico</a>, failed in April 1797. As Commander-in-Chief in Ireland in 1798 he publicly criticised the army's indiscipline under his predecessor, the 2nd Earl of Carhampton, sought to restore civil authority and resigned for lack of support. In 1799 he led the landing at Callantsoog in the Anglo-Russian expedition to North Holland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1801 he landed his army at Aboukir against strong opposition. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 21 March the French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-francois-menou">Jacques-François Menou</a> attacked before dawn; Abercromby was briefly captured by French dragoons and rescued by a highlander of the 42nd, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a>-ball struck his thigh. He stayed in command until the battle was won, was carried aboard HMS Foudroyant and died on 28 March 1801. He was buried in Fort Saint Elmo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a>, where St John's Bastion was renamed Abercrombie's Bastion. Parliament voted him a monument in St Paul's Cathedral, and his widow was created Baroness Abercromby.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ralph Abercromby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In February 1797 a British force under <b>Sir Ralph Abercromby</b> captured the island almost without resistance, and the Treaty of Amiens in 1802 confirmed the cession.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-trinidad">Colony of Trinidad</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1797 a British force under <b>Ralph Abercromby</b> captured the island, and Governor José María Chacón surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tullibody, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.14,-3.84&amp;year=1777" aria-label="Tullibody on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ralph Abercromby"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4817 5325h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4817 5325h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.14,-3.84&amp;year=1777">What was here in 1777? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
